---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Penguin-Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';

const currentDate = new Date();
let currentYear = currentDate.getFullYear();
---

<Layout title="Penguins">
	<head>
		<meta charset="UTF-8">
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
		<title>Emperor Penguins</title>
	</head>
	<body>
		<header class="header">
        	<h1>Emperor Penguin</h1>
    	</header>
		<Navbar/>
        <main>
            <div class="container">
                <nav class="contents">
                    <h2>Contents:</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#diet">Diet</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#behavior">Social Behavior</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#facts">Facts</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </nav>
                
                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Scientific Name: Aptenodytes forsteri</li>
                        <li>Size: The largest penguin species, standing 100–120 cm (39–47 inches) tall and weighing 25–40 kg (55–88 lb).</li>
                        <li>Habitat: Breed on the sea ice of Antarctica during the harsh winter months.</li>
                        <li>Conservation Status: Least Concern (IUCN)</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                <section id="diet">
                    <h2>Diet</h2>
                    <p>Emperor penguins are fish specialists, primarily feeding on squid, fish, and krill.  They are deep-diving predators, capable of reaching depths of up to 565 meters (1,854 feet) to hunt for food.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="behavior">
                    <h2>Social Behavior</h2>
                    <p>Emperor penguins are highly social creatures, forming large breeding colonies that can number in the tens of thousands.  During the harsh Antarctic winter, they huddle together in dense groups to conserve heat and survive the extreme conditions.  Emperor penguins are monogamous birds, forming pairs that often remain together for multiple breeding seasons.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="facts">
                    <h2>Interesting Facts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Emperor penguins are the only birds that breed in the harsh Antarctic winter.</li>
                        <li>They are known for their impressive endurance, traveling hundreds of kilometers across the ice to reach their breeding colonies.</li>
                        <li>Emperor penguins have a unique breeding strategy, with females laying a single egg that is passed from male to male during the incubation period.</li>
                        <li>These penguins are an important indicator species for the health of the Antarctic ecosystem. Changes in their population can signal problems in the food chain or the environment.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
            <footer>
                <p><span class="copyleft">&copy;</span> PengiCoder & MrPiggy105, { currentYear }</p>
            </footer>
        </main>
	</body>
</Layout>

<style>
    .contents ul {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .contents li {
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    section {
        overflow: hidden;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        margin-left: 30%;
    }

    h1{
        text-align: center;
        height: 100%;
        font-family: 'Orkney', Arial;
    }
</style>
